WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.360
A million-dollar garage, weird&nbsp;
rivalries, and who are we fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.920
anyway? You might feel a need for speed&nbsp;
after watching the "Top Gun" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.800
but you’ll also be left with&nbsp;
more questions than answers.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.520
Integral to the ethos of the "Top Gun" movies and&nbsp;
the TOPGUN school is the idea that these pilot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:26.240
trainees are the best of the best. It's where the&nbsp;
top 1% of new pilots go to study the most advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.840
flying maneuvers. But at the end of "Top Gun," a&nbsp;
few of our brand-new class of graduates are called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.360
away urgently for a "crisis situation." In "Top&nbsp;
Gun: Maverick," the entire movie is based around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.200
teaching recent TOPGUN grads to fly a dangerous,&nbsp;
urgent assignment to destroy a nuclear facility.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:53.567
Maverick ends up leading the mission himself in&nbsp;
"Top Gun: Maverick," of course, but are there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:58.847
no other veterans out there with any chops at all?&nbsp;
In the first "Top Gun," the cadets don't even have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.847 --> 00:01:03.807
time to enjoy their graduation banquet. The plot&nbsp;
intervenes to place them in a deadly situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.807 --> 00:01:10.127
that just doesn't seem like an actual entry-level&nbsp;
assignment, even for the "best of the best."

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:15.887
The most cognitively dissonant part of the&nbsp;
first "Top Gun" is the relationship between our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.887 --> 00:01:21.487
protagonist Maverick and his arch-rival, Iceman.&nbsp;
If you just read the dialogue in their scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.487 --> 00:01:26.367
you might assume Iceman is actually the lead&nbsp;
role, because every single thing he sneers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:31.407
at his reckless nemesis is actually just&nbsp;
a very sensible thing to point out. Iceman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.407 --> 00:01:37.327
is described as a talented and polished pilot,&nbsp;
and he points out multiple times that Maverick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.327 --> 00:01:43.247
acts irresponsibly by defying protocol and taking&nbsp;
needless risks. The way the scenes are acted, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.247 --> 00:01:48.847
the tension that Val Kilmer and Tom Cruise capably&nbsp;
bring to their roles, make you more likely to root&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.847 --> 00:01:55.407
for Maverick just out of movie-watching habit.&nbsp;
Why is this blond jerk being so snide to our hero?

00:01:55.407 --> 00:02:00.607
The thing is, Iceman is right:&nbsp;
Maverick is talented but pure chaos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.607 --> 00:02:05.167
and even his superiors admit they have&nbsp;
grave doubts about how his attitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:09.727
will translate to combat. Maverick&nbsp;
even indirectly gets Goose killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.727 --> 00:02:15.007
and Iceman takes the high road and offers his&nbsp;
sympathy instead of saying "I told you so."

00:02:16.927 --> 00:02:20.687
"Mitchell, I'm sorry about Goose."

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:25.327
At the end of "Top Gun," Maverick&nbsp;
saves Iceman's life in combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.327 --> 00:02:29.167
and they become lifelong friends,&nbsp;
so all's well that ends well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:34.447
But no one brings up that Maverick learned&nbsp;
nothing. He ignored Iceman's level-headed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.447 --> 00:02:38.927
reasonable advice, and it all just kind of&nbsp;
worked out for the best in spite of him.

00:02:39.807 --> 00:02:45.567
Any moviegoer that's seen a war movie is familiar&nbsp;
with the iconography of military dog tags.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:50.607
In countless scenes, they're recovered from&nbsp;
the fallen as a symbolic gesture of carrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.607 --> 00:02:56.207
the memory of their service, and in due course&nbsp;
passed on to the soldier's next-of-kin. Why then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.207 --> 00:03:01.247
in "Top Gun," does Maverick keep Goose's dog&nbsp;
tags instead of giving them to his widow?

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:05.887
At first, distraught as he is with grief,&nbsp;
it's understandable that he would hold onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.887 --> 00:03:10.047
them as he pondered whether to continue&nbsp;
his career as a pilot. But ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.047 --> 00:03:14.447
after the big climatic dogfight, Maverick&nbsp;
cathartically throws them into the ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.447 --> 00:03:19.807
ensuring Goose's family will never get them.&nbsp;
It could be argued that this is in keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.807 --> 00:03:24.527
with Maverick's rather self-centered nature,&nbsp;
but "Top Gun" lets us know with the score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.527 --> 00:03:29.647
and general tone of the scene that it&nbsp;
considers this a meaningful, thoughtful moment.

00:03:30.687 --> 00:03:35.887
Maverick's appeal in both "Top Gun" movies&nbsp;
is a self-fulfilling paradox. He's the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.887 --> 00:03:42.127
character and beautiful women are drawn to him, so&nbsp;
presumably he must be charming. But that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.127 --> 00:03:47.967
hold up when you watch his actual interactions&nbsp;
with Charlie Blackwood, played by Kelly McGillis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:53.567
in "Top Gun." First, after a mildly amusing but&nbsp;
also embarrassing impromptu karaoke session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:58.607
he follows into the women's bathroom to make&nbsp;
a fool of himself and aggressively hit on her.

00:03:58.607 --> 00:04:03.487
Instead of being even momentarily&nbsp;
cowed or apologetic when he realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.487 --> 00:04:07.407
she's actually a TOPGUN instructor,&nbsp;
Maverick interrupts her presentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.407 --> 00:04:12.447
and embarrasses her again in front of all&nbsp;
the other recruits. There is some vague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.447 --> 00:04:17.167
pillow talk about how she's impressed by&nbsp;
his flying skills, and wants to hear more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:21.967
about the inverted maneuver with an enemy MiG he&nbsp;
pulls off in the beginning of the movie. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.967 --> 00:04:27.807
their romance largely unfolds with a dreamlike&nbsp;
inevitability. It's almost as if they conclude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.807 --> 00:04:33.407
that they should fall in love just because they&nbsp;
both look like very attractive movie stars.

00:04:34.287 --> 00:04:39.807
Early in "Top Gun" it's insinuated that Maverick&nbsp;
has succeeded in spite of the reputation of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.807 --> 00:04:45.247
late father, himself a pilot who died during&nbsp;
the Vietnam War under mysterious circumstances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.247 --> 00:04:49.007
Much like how he would later work&nbsp;
against Rooster in "Top Gun: Maverick,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.007 --> 00:04:53.567
Maverick is denied entry to the naval academy&nbsp;
because of Duke Mitchell's negative reputation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:57.727
implying that the military holds grudges&nbsp;
for more than a generation. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.727 --> 00:05:02.767
Commander Mike "Viper" Mitchell reveals&nbsp;
to Maverick that his father died a hero.

00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:05.727
"His F-4 was hit. He was wounded&nbsp;
but he could have made it back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.727 --> 00:05:08.447
He stayed in it. Saved three&nbsp;
planes before he bought it."

00:05:09.007 --> 00:05:14.367
This quick explanation raises a multitude of&nbsp;
questions that Maverick doesn't bother to ask.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:18.287
How does a wounded man in a damaged&nbsp;
plane save three other planes?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.287 --> 00:05:22.607
Viper says it was all covered up because it&nbsp;
happened on the wrong side of a border, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.607 --> 00:05:27.407
doesn't explain why the elder Mitchell ended&nbsp;
up being blamed for his own death instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.407 --> 00:05:32.127
lauded as a life-saving hero. Maverick's&nbsp;
surface-level acceptance of a secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.127 --> 00:05:36.447
he's waited his entire life to learn&nbsp;
just makes it all the more confusing.

00:05:37.407 --> 00:05:41.487
"Top Gun: Maverick" trades the shallow,&nbsp;
glossy romance of the first movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.487 --> 00:05:45.807
for a deeper, more lived-in love story&nbsp;
as he reconnects with Penny Benjamin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.807 --> 00:05:50.287
played by Jennifer Connelly. Penny Benjamin&nbsp;
is referenced in "Top Gun" as the "admiral's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.287 --> 00:05:55.807
daughter" that Maverick had a dalliance with,&nbsp;
among his many other insubordinations. It adds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.807 --> 00:06:00.287
an element of nostalgic sweetness as the&nbsp;
characters bond over paternal struggles.

00:06:00.287 --> 00:06:05.167
Bit by bit, it's revealed or implied that&nbsp;
Penny and Maverick have gotten together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:10.367
split up several times over the years. Indeed,&nbsp;
if they first became involved before the events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:14.367
shown in "Top Gun," by the time "Top Gun:&nbsp;
Maverick" takes place they've been on again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:19.807
off again for nearly four decades. Penny's&nbsp;
daughter even somewhat coldly warns Maverick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.807 --> 00:06:24.047
not to break her mother's heart again. Though&nbsp;
the actors have chemistry, the vague references&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.047 --> 00:06:28.927
they make to a shared past don't really seem&nbsp;
to reflect the actual amount of time that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.927 --> 00:06:33.887
characters have known one another. They pick&nbsp;
up where they left off entirely too easily, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.887 --> 00:06:39.087
never have to hash out what drove them apart&nbsp;
in the past at any length or in any depth.

00:06:40.047 --> 00:06:44.687
The biggest gaping hole in the screenplay of&nbsp;
both "Top Gun" movies is the identity of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.687 --> 00:06:51.087
ever-mysterious "enemy." Devotees and armchair&nbsp;
sleuths have various explanations for both movies:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.087 --> 00:06:55.727
in short, based on a few clues, it's&nbsp;
likely either Russia or China in "Top Gun,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.727 --> 00:06:58.607
and could more or less only be Iran in "Top Gun:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.607 --> 00:07:03.327
Maverick." But the movies themselves dance around&nbsp;
the topic with an unsubtle deliberateness that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.327 --> 00:07:08.127
requires us as viewers to just quiet down&nbsp;
the basic deductive parts of our brains.

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:12.847
In "Top Gun," the "enemy" is only represented&nbsp;
by a few MiG fighters seen in action sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.847 --> 00:07:16.767
so you don't have much time to wonder about&nbsp;
their point of origin. But in "Top Gun:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.767 --> 00:07:20.447
Maverick," we see extensive, clear&nbsp;
footage of the enemy nation's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.447 --> 00:07:26.367
"5th generation" advanced fighter jets — the&nbsp;
pilots of which wear conveniently tinted visors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.367 --> 00:07:31.007
Later, Maverick and Rooster run onto an&nbsp;
enemy airfield and steal one of their planes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.007 --> 00:07:36.207
all without catching a glimpse of a human&nbsp;
face or any kind of telltale flag or insignia.

00:07:37.167 --> 00:07:41.247
Ultimately neither "Top Gun" movie can&nbsp;
afford to identify the enemy nation involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.247 --> 00:07:45.567
because to do so would risk inflaming&nbsp;
international tensions in the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:50.607
world and raise impossible questions to answer&nbsp;
in their fictional one. Namely, wouldn't the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.607 --> 00:07:55.807
climax of both movies rise to the level of&nbsp;
an incident that could incite World War III?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.807 --> 00:08:00.047
In "Top Gun," set in the waning days of the&nbsp;
Cold War, shooting down a few MiG fighters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.047 --> 00:08:05.167
could perhaps be reasonably settled with whatever&nbsp;
country they came from without further escalation.

00:08:05.167 --> 00:08:09.887
It's "Top Gun: Maverick" that truly makes&nbsp;
no sense. The nation that America is so keen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.887 --> 00:08:14.287
to prevent from enriching uranium may&nbsp;
not have nuclear capability just yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.287 --> 00:08:19.247
but it has sophisticated surface-to-air missiles.&nbsp;
They also have 5th generation fighter planes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.247 --> 00:08:23.887
are superior to what Maverick and co. are&nbsp;
flying. Such a nation would definitely have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.887 --> 00:08:29.087
long-range missiles that would lay waste to&nbsp;
our heroes' entire aircraft carrier more or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.087 --> 00:08:34.127
less immediately after the mission, and&nbsp;
presumably incite all-out war from there.

00:08:35.087 --> 00:08:40.607
The years have apparently been very kind to Pete&nbsp;
"Maverick" Mitchell between "Top Gun" and "Top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.607 --> 00:08:45.727
Gun: Maverick." He barely looks any older, he&nbsp;
still gets to fly planes, and he's stretched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.727 --> 00:08:51.887
every penny of his Captain's salary to the limit&nbsp;
as he owns two absurdly expensive vehicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.887 --> 00:08:58.207
as does his love interest Penny Benjamin. Mav has&nbsp;
his own personal hanger with a vintage P-51 plane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.207 --> 00:09:04.847
worth $4.5 million, as well as a vintage green&nbsp;
Aston Martin worth more than $22 million.

00:09:05.487 --> 00:09:10.927
Not to be outdone, the bar that Penny owns&nbsp;
is either incomprehensibly successful or her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.927 --> 00:09:16.607
admiral father has helped her out considerably.&nbsp;
She owns a sailboat valued at more than $300,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.247 --> 00:09:23.887
and shows up at the end of the movie in a Porsche&nbsp;
worth $181,000. Maverick must have a type: in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.887 --> 00:09:29.327
the original "Top Gun" his love interest Charlie&nbsp;
drove a similarly-priced black Porsche speedster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.327 --> 00:09:34.447
as well. It's hard not to wonder: how do all&nbsp;
these characters afford such expensive vehicles?

00:09:35.087 --> 00:09:40.047
"Just wanna manage expectations."

